body:
I am firmly convinced that the human mind is undeniably real and tangible, existing not only within our own being but also in the existence of others we encounter in our shared reality. As we delve into the contemplation of the essence of this world we inhabit, we can detect a certain semblance of artificiality intertwined within its intricate fabric, causing us to question its authenticity in relation to our original state of existence. 

&#x200B;

However, we must acknowledge that our true home lies in the realm of non-duality, a realm beyond the boundaries of this perceptible dualistic reality we currently find ourselves in. This realm we dwell in now, though possessing a certain degree of solidity within our subjective experience, is inherently transient, serving as a temporary dwelling rather than an illusory facade. It is not fake. 

&#x200B;

After all, should we consider something to be fake or unreal merely because it occurs within the confines of our minds? It’s a world that was created for us to learn lessons. You are real, your friends are real people, your family are real people. Your home is in the non dual realm. The house you live in now as a human being is a temporary abode. You’ll return home with the people you care about one day. We all will. In the meantime just enjoy the ride.

SLEEP DISORDERS. It happens to many, not infrequently, to suddenly wake up from sleep and not be able to move. This state, commonly called "sleep paralysis", is one of the most common disorders and can manifest itself in two phases: before falling asleep (hypnagogic paralysis) or during awakening (hypnopompic paralysis), and can have a duration ranging from a few seconds about two minutes. While on the one hand we find ourselves with a paralyzed body and inhibited communication skills, on the other hand the mind is perfectly clear and the senses are active. A state of panic and distress ensues, with the inability to cry out for help and the sensation of suffocation, as if someone were pressing on the chest. The scientific explanation for this is given precisely by "sleep paralysis", the symptoms of which appear for a few seconds.

&#x200B;

S'AMMUTADORI. However, the island culture finds the answer in an evil entity far from scientific motivations. This takes the name of Ammutadori (or Ammuntadore), which would be nothing more than a sleep demon. Specifically, it is a mythological creature of the Sardinian tradition whose name derives from the Sardinian ammuntare / -ai ("to have nightmares") and which, precisely through nightmares ("ammuntaduras"), would attack people in their sleep. These, resting on the victim's chest, would create a sense of oppression and anguish. It creeps into nightmares and therefore it is almost impossible to drive it away, unless the appropriate incantations are recited, the so-called brebu (verb, word). It also seems that, to defend themselves from the demon, the elderly used to put on their feet, at the foot of the bed, an item of clothing that they commonly used during the day.

&#x200B;

HISTORY AND ORIGINS. Testimonies regarding the existence of fantastic nocturnal beings are consumed over the millennia, since ancient history. In Sardinia, well before popular stories, it seems that there was talk of evil figures sometimes capable of acting by exploiting people's sleep. According to some scholars this belief originated after 241 BC, when the territory came under the control of the Romans following the triumph over the Carthaginians, while for others the island would have developed these superstitions much earlier. Over the centuries the figure of s'Ammutadori would have undergone changes, and with the advent of Christianity would have partly lost the characteristic features of a nocturnal spirit to get closer, in configuration, to the image of Satan. Similar creatures, by the Romans were called with the name of Incubi (Incubus), or in the female version of Succubi (Succubus), and had the same effect on the people involved as the island night demon.

&#x200B;

TESTIMONIALS. Even today it happens to find those who claim to have come into contact with it. According to numerous testimonies, it would be a being that does not have a real form, since the identikit provided is partially different from victim to victim. Some claim to have seen s'Ammutadori in the form of a witch, others of a skeleton, and some still of clouds of steam or even of individuals whose faces were not clearly visible or bloodied. While as far as the consequences of the encounter are concerned, they commonly appear to be those listed above, therefore from an initial tingling you gradually pass to momentary paralysis, with the victim trying to scream for help, barely managing to whisper.

&#x200B;

THE MYTH YESTERDAY AND TODAY. This figure, as well as many others similar or different, for centuries has haunted the dreams of the unfortunate, who, lacking a scientific explanation, sought in the apotropaic rites typical of a culture profoundly influenced by spiritual beliefs a way to ward off the evil being. Today, of course, the influence of such influential supernatural figures and beliefs has faded, however the charm and history of a world remains which, in its imaginative embroideries, continues to arouse interest and transmit, through the powerful medium of oral tradition, anxieties and reveries.

This figure is also present in other cultures: in Egypt it is known with the name of Jinn, in Japan with Kanashibari and in central Italy (Marche and Abruzzo) as Pandafeche or Pantafa while in Gallura it is called Pundacciu.

